<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Oceans-7 on Corey Murphey</title><link>https://c-murph-swim.github.io/tags/oceans-7/</link><description>Recent content in Oceans-7 on Corey Murphey</description><generator>Hugo -- gohugo.io</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Tue, 12 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://c-murph-swim.github.io/tags/oceans-7/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Strait of Gibraltar</title><link>https://c-murph-swim.github.io/p/gibraltar-2026/</link><pubDate>Tue, 12 May 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://c-murph-swim.github.io/p/gibraltar-2026/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="strait-of-gibraltar---may-11-2026"&gt;Strait of Gibraltar - May 11, 2026
&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;On Easter, my dear friend, Sue Mckay, texted to ask if I wanted to join her Gibraltar pod. I met Sue last year at SCAR, and we had kept in touch, bonding over our insane decision to swim the North Channel this year (iykyk).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Gibraltar has a notoriously long waitlist unless you’re asked to join a group. While Gibraltar was not on the plan for this year, being the rational and non-impulsive person I am, I immediately started looking for flights. Going into this swim, I told myself I was going for “vibes”. I was there to swim with friends across a gorgeous but unpredictable strip of water. And it far exceeded all expectations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In a nearly unheard of series of events, we swam on the first day of our window. While the conditions were going to be rough at the start, we knew the current would be in our favor if we got far enough out in the first hour. Starting at sunrise, we had a bumpy start and were unable to safely touch the rocks near Tarifa. Nikko, Marcelo, Sue, and I hammered for the first hour as instructed. The sunrise was magical, our skin shimmering in the crystal clear water. I’ve never swum through water so clear that I didn’t really have to sight forward ever. I could clearly see Sue, Marcelo, and Nikko during each stroke. I was sighting so infrequently that I nearly swam head first into a turtle!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is a relatively “short” (by my standards) swim at 15.1km. After the first hour, Sue and I were completely in sync. Many giggles were shared as we gleefully declared we have many tandem swims in our future. We were so happy to be swimming in such gorgeous water at a comfortable pace with a substantial push at our feet guiding us toward Cires, Morocco. I will be forever grateful for this swim and for Nikko, Sue, and Marcelo for sharing this experience with me.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A massive thanks to ACNEG for crewing for us all. To my Gibraltar pod, Marcelo Dietric, Nikko Price, and Sue for including me in this adventure. And to Sue and Liam for putting up with a near stranger for 12 days (Sue and I are actually the same person if you didn’t know it). 💙&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Catalina Channel 2025</title><link>https://c-murph-swim.github.io/p/catalina-2025/</link><pubDate>Mon, 08 Sep 2025 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://c-murph-swim.github.io/p/catalina-2025/</guid><description>&lt;img src="https://c-murph-swim.github.io/p/catalina-2025/figs/everyone.jpeg" alt="Featured image of post Catalina Channel 2025" /&gt;&lt;h1 id="catalina-channel-crossing----august-7-8-2025"&gt;Catalina Channel Crossing - August 7-8, 2025
&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p align="center"&gt;
&lt;img alt="Swimming with the dolphins" src="figs/neil+me+dolphins.jpeg" width="100%"&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Almost a year in the making and it did not disappoint. I had the most amazing crew: Helen, Max, Jake, and Lauren of the Pacific Star; my kayakers Neil van der Byl and John Trevino; my crew…mom (Jill Murphey), Amie Freeman, and Zach Brown (also my support swimmer); and my observers Alex Pine and Elaine van Vleck.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This swim got off to a rough start with an embarrassing bout of seasickness and large swells for the first few hours of the swim. I got through it mostly because I just didn’t want to get back on the boat and get sick again, not by some extreme feat of strength or bravery, believe me… But I was greeted with one of the most magical sunrises ever: several schools of dolphins, a seal that swam right under me, jellyfish, fish, worms, and a friendly face in Zach swimming with me at dawn. And I even swam right over a seal in the final minute of this swim at the most beautiful finish beach… a sight for sore eyes. This swim is both tough and rewarding at the same time. I cannot say I loved every minute of it, but I will surely remember it with fondness — because I was surrounded by beautiful people, creatures, views, and water 💙. Somehow this kid from central Missouri is an ocean swimmer now!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I am so grateful to my crew, my coaches (Neil Hailstone and de facto coach/open water bible/swim buddy Sarah Thomas), Boulder Aquatic Masters, my pilates teacher Erica Ruge, my PT Jim Heafner, training buddies, and countless friends and family who picked me up and encouraged me along this journey. Love you all 💙&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A final note about the song in this reel: a few weeks ago my friend, Tim Stumbaugh invited me to a Jack&amp;rsquo;s Mannequin at Red Rocks. The song &amp;ldquo;Swim&amp;rdquo; has been on my mind ever since. And it helped me through the toughest parts of this swim. I thought a lot about both the literal and figurative meanings of the lyrics —and it was so relevant at every moment. Hence it stayed with me all 9 hours. This song was important to me when it was released many (many) moons ago and it still is apparently. So yay for nostalgic music that plays on repeat for almost 9 hours in your head. And yay for working through tough moments any way you can (even when it’s not pretty).&lt;/p&gt;
